<p>Set two spheres against one another and wrap them in a third, as shown. Now add a new sphere, tangent to the first two and to the large enclosing sphere. Surprisingly, if you keep adding these new spheres, tangent to one another, they&#8217;ll always form a &#8220;necklace&#8221; of six.</p>
<p>English radiochemist Frederick Soddy discovered this pretty fact in 1937, though it had been known to Japanese mathematicians since at least 1822. Soddy, who had written <a href="https://www.futilitycloset.com/2014/06/19/descartes-theorem/">a paean to Descartes&#8217; theorem</a> in <em>Nature</em> in June 1936, added another that December to celebrate his finding:</p>
<p>However ill-assorted in girth three spheres may be<br />
Each one can kiss the other two simultaneously<br />
A ring of six about them all kissing serially.</p>
<p>Though any necklet of graded beads<br />
May fit in general the she-sex,<br />
This hexlet of mine of novel design<br />
Caresses not one but three necks.<br />
However it&#8217;s worn it alters its grade<br />
To suit its tri-spherical prison,<br />
Plays kiss-in-the-ring and merry-go-round<br />
Whilst hugging three necks with precision.<br />
Like bubbles that blow and kindle and go<br />
It holds up to light-hearted derision<br />
Makes of the pure circumflex<br />
And its pet aversion in the mental inversion<br />
That will have &#8220;It&#8217;s 1/<em>x</em>.&#8221;</p>
<p>All saints and sages throughout the ages<br />
From one doxy never have swerved,<br />
To hold fast unto what in change changes not<br />
And ferret out what is conserved.<br />
Now these beads without flaw obey this first law<br />
For the aggregate sum of their bends.<br />
As each in the tunnel slims through the funnel<br />
Its <em>vis-à-vis</em> grossly distends.<br />
<em>But the mean of the bends of each opposite pair<br />
Is the sum of the three of the thoroughfare.</em></p>
